# The one NPC in the harness that can fight.
#
# The tester cannot: its role is built on Template_Temple, whose attitude sensors cover Neutral and Friendly
# and which has no hostile branch, no attack and no combat. So <<attitude hostile>> on it changes a value
# nothing reads, and "does a hostile NPC behave like one" was unanswerable there.
#
# A goblin scavenger answers it. Two things learned by running it, both of which shape this file:
#
#   1. It does nothing while the window is open. Hostility is only visible once the conversation closes, so
#      the check ends by handing control back rather than by showing anything.
#   2. So it cannot put itself back at the end of the same passage. <<wait>> was tried and is worse than
#      useless here: going hostile means attacking, being attacked closes the window, and everything after
#      the wait is abandoned with it. Calming down is an option the tester picks, when they have seen enough.
#   3. And an attitude alone does not stop it. An attitude decides who an NPC starts on, not a fight already
#      under way; <<attitude friendly>> ran twice on it mid-swing and it kept swinging. That is what
#      <<calm>> was added to LowTalk for -- it clears the targets the role has marked -- and this file is
#      what tests it. Despawning is still offered as the way out if it does not work.

npc: @harness_fighter
title: LowTalk harness (fighter)
speaker: Scavenger

== start
I can actually fight, which the tester cannot. My attitude to you is {attitude()}.
-> Turn hostile <<if not (attitude() == "hostile")>>
    <<jump check_attitude_behaviour>>
-> Make me friendly again <<if attitude() == "hostile">>
    <<jump calm>>
-> Send me away
    <<jump leave>>
-> Nothing for now
    <<end>>

== check_attitude_behaviour
<<if not player_detectable()>>
    <<jump cannot_see_you>>
<<endif>>
<<attitude hostile>>
<<if attitude() == "hostile">>
    <<jump check_attitude_behaviour_ok>>
<<else>>
    <<jump check_attitude_behaviour_bad>>
<<endif>>

== check_attitude_behaviour_ok
Hostile, though you would not know it from in here: I do nothing while we are talking. Close this window and watch. Then talk to me again and make me friendly. You will take a few hits; I will patch you up afterwards.
-> Leave me to it
    <<end>>
-> On second thoughts, make me friendly again
    <<jump calm>>

== check_attitude_behaviour_bad
Wrong, or the assertion is. attitude() reads {attitude()} rather than hostile.
-> Calm down
    <<jump calm>>

== calm
<<attitude friendly>>
<<calm>>
My attitude reads {attitude()} and I have forgotten what I was fighting. The attitude alone would not have done it: that decides who I start on, not a fight I am already in. If I am still swinging, say so with /harness feedback and send me away.
-> Good
    <<end>>
-> Send me away anyway
    <<jump leave>>

== cannot_see_you
I cannot see you at all -- you are in creative, and NPCs do not notice creative players unless the world says otherwise. Nothing I do now would prove anything, so I have not recorded this check. Switch to adventure or survival and ask me again.
-> Back
    <<jump start>>

== leave
Right you are.
<<calm>>
<<heal>>
<<despawn>>
